{name:"Hessonite",kind:"gem",price:"$20–$150 / ct",img:"images/hessonite.jpg",formula:"Ca₃Al₂(SiO₄)₃",class:"Silicate — Garnet",system:"Cubic",mohs:7.25,sg:"3.65",ri:"1.74",colorHex:["#c07030","#8a4818"],stone:"#c07030",glow:"rgba(192,112,48,0.5)",colors:["Cinnamon","Honey orange"],origins:["Sri Lanka","India","Brazil","Madagascar"],desc:"The warm cinnamon-to-honey variety of grossular garnet. Its name comes from the Greek for amber-like resin. Clean faceted hessonite is a classic collector and jewelry stone, with a soft glow rather than sharp diamond-like sparkle."},
{name:"Rhodolite",kind:"gem",price:"$50–$400 / ct",img:"images/rhodolite.jpg",formula:"(Mg,Fe)₃Al₂(SiO₄)₃",class:"Silicate — Garnet",system:"Cubic",mohs:7.5,sg:"3.84",ri:"1.76",colorHex:["#a02858","#701838"],stone:"#a02858",glow:"rgba(160,40,88,0.5)",colors:["Raspberry","Purplish red"],origins:["Tanzania","Sri Lanka","India","USA"],desc:"A raspberry-to-purplish-red pyrope–almandine garnet blend prized for its lively color. The name means rose stone. Fine rhodolite is a favorite alternative to ruby among gem buyers who want rich red without the corundum price."},
{name:"Dravite",kind:"gem",price:"$20–$200 / ct",img:"images/dravite.jpg",formula:"NaMg₃Al₆(Si₆O₁₈)(BO₃)₃(OH)₃OH",class:"Silicate — Tourmaline",system:"Trigonal",mohs:7.25,sg:"3.10",ri:"1.63",colorHex:["#6a4828","#3a2814"],stone:"#6a4828",glow:"rgba(106,72,40,0.5)",colors:["Brown","Yellow-brown"],origins:["Austria","Australia","Brazil","USA"],desc:"The brown magnesium tourmaline end-member, named for the Drava river region. Crystals are often stout trigonal prisms. Transparent golden-brown dravite is cut as a collector gem; darker material is common in metamorphic rocks."},
{name:"Liddicoatite",kind:"gem",price:"$100–$1,500 / ct",img:"images/liddicoatite.jpg",formula:"Ca(Li,Al)₃Al₆(Si₆O₁₈)(BO₃)₃(OH)₃F",class:"Silicate — Tourmaline",system:"Trigonal",mohs:7.25,sg:"3.05",ri:"1.63",colorHex:["#c04070","#2f8a4a"],stone:"#a05070",glow:"rgba(160,80,112,0.5)",colors:["Pink","Green","Zoned"],origins:["Madagascar","Canada","Brazil"],desc:"A calcium-rich tourmaline famous for spectacular color zoning — slices can look like stained-glass flowers. Named for GIA’s Richard T. Liddicoat. Fine Madagascar slices are among the most collected tourmaline specimens."},
{name:"Selenite",kind:"gem",price:"$2–$30 / piece",img:"images/selenite.jpg",formula:"CaSO₄·2H₂O",class:"Sulfate — Gypsum",system:"Monoclinic",mohs:2,sg:"2.32",ri:"1.53",colorHex:["#f2f0e8","#d0ccc0"],stone:"#ebe8e0",glow:"rgba(235,232,224,0.5)",colors:["Colorless","White"],origins:["Mexico","USA","Spain","Poland"],desc:"Clear crystalline gypsum — soft enough to scratch with a fingernail, yet famous for huge transparent blades and desert-rose clusters. The name nods to the moon-like pearly luster. Handle gently; it is a display classic, not a ring stone."},
{name:"Londonite",kind:"gem",price:"$200–$2,000 / ct",img:"images/londonite.jpg",formula:"(Cs,K,Rb)Al₄Be₄(B,Be)₁₂O₂₈",class:"Borate",system:"Cubic",mohs:8,sg:"3.34",ri:"1.69",colorHex:["#e8d860","#a09020"],stone:"#e0d050",glow:"rgba(224,208,80,0.5)",colors:["Yellow","Greenish yellow"],origins:["Madagascar"],desc:"A rare cesium-rich borate gem, the Cs-dominant counterpart to rhodizite. Named for David London. Faceted yellow londonite from Madagascar is a serious collector’s stone — hard, bright, and seldom seen."},
{name:"Rhodizite",kind:"gem",price:"$100–$1,000 / ct",img:"images/rhodizite.jpg",formula:"(K,Cs)Al₄Be₄(B,Be)₁₂O₂₈",class:"Borate",system:"Cubic",mohs:8,sg:"3.40",ri:"1.69",colorHex:["#e8e0a0","#b0a860"],stone:"#e0d890",glow:"rgba(224,216,144,0.5)",colors:["Yellow","Colorless","Grey"],origins:["Madagascar","Russia"],desc:"A hard, rare borate that forms sharp isometric crystals. Often associated with londonite in Madagascar pegmatites. Transparent yellow stones are uncommon and sought by collectors of exotic gems."},
{name:"Heulandite",kind:"gem",price:"$5–$40 / piece",img:"images/heulandite.jpg",formula:"(Ca,Na)₂₋₃Al₃(Al,Si)₂Si₁₃O₃₆·12H₂O",class:"Silicate — Zeolite",system:"Monoclinic",mohs:3.75,sg:"2.20",ri:"1.50",colorHex:["#c89070","#8a6040"],stone:"#c89070",glow:"rgba(200,144,112,0.5)",colors:["Pink","White","Green"],origins:["Iceland","India","USA","Italy"],desc:"A zeolite famous for coffin-shaped crystals and pearly cleavage faces. Pink heulandite from India and Iceland is a staple of teaching collections. Soft and fragile — admired as specimens rather than jewelry."},
{name:"Clinochlore",kind:"gem",price:"$5–$60 / ct",img:"images/clinochlore.jpg",formula:"Mg₅Al(AlSi₃O₁₀)(OH)₈",class:"Silicate — Chlorite",system:"Monoclinic",mohs:2.5,sg:"2.65",ri:"1.58",colorHex:["#2f8a5a","#1a5234"],stone:"#2f8a5a",glow:"rgba(47,138,90,0.5)",colors:["Green","Silvery green"],origins:["Russia","Switzerland","USA","Italy"],desc:"A green chlorite-group mineral. Chatoyant silvery-green material from the Kola Peninsula is sold as seraphinite. Soft and cleavable, it is cut as cabochons and carvings rather than faceted gems."}
